:"Maybank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":5.421821,"lo":100.335416,"d":1142},{"n":"Swettenham Pier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.418933,"lo":100.346329,"d":180,"ds":"Swettenham Pier is a pier within the city of George Town in the Malaysian state of Penang. Established in 1904, it is the busiest port-of-call in Malaysia for cruise shipping. The pier plays a vital role as a major entry point for tourists into Penang, aside from the Penang International Airport and land connections.","wpu":"Swettenham Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Swettenham_Pier_in_George_Town%2C_Penang_2023.jpg/960px-Swettenham_Pier_in_George_Town%2C_Penang_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"OCBC Building (Penang)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.41725,"lo":100.34192,"d":351,"ds":"The OCBC Building is a historical building in George Town within the Malaysian state of Penang. Completed in 1938, the office building, situated at Beach Street within the city's Central Business District (CBD), forms part of the Penang branch of the OCBC Bank.","wpu":"OCBC Building (Penang)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/OCBC_Bank_Building_at_Beach_Street_in_George_Town%2C_Penang.jpg/960px-OCBC_Bank_Building_at_Beach_Street_in_George_Town%2C_Penang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"City Hall, George Town","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.4219,"lo":100.3414,"d":588,"ds":"The City Hall is the local government headquarters of George Town within the Malaysian state of Penang. Built by the British, it now serves as the seat of the Penang Island City Council and was previously the seat of the George Town City Council. Originally completed in 1903 as the Municipal Offices, the building was erected at a cost of $100,000 to relieve the demand for office space at the adjacent Town Hall.","wpu":"City Hall, George Town","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Penang_City_Hall_%28I%29.jpg/960px-Penang_City_Hall_%28I%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Francis Light Memorial","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.420028,"lo":100.339306,"d":669,"ds":"The Francis Light Memorial is a memorial about Francis Light in George Town, Penang, Malaysia. The memorial, located underneath the dome, reads: \"In memory of Francis Light ESQ who first established this island as an English settlement and was many years governor. Born in the county of Suffolk in England and died October 21st, 1794.","wpu":"Francis Light Memorial","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Penang_Malaysia_Francis-Light-Memorial-01.jpg/960px-Penang_Malaysia_Francis-Light-Memorial-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nagore Durgha Sheriff","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.41627,"lo":100.3389,"d":702,"ds":"The Nagore Durgha Sheriff is an Sufi dargah complex incorporating a Sufi shrine and mausoleum, that was completed in the early 19th century, located at the junction of Chulia Street and King Street, George Town, in Penang, Malaysia.","wpu":"Nagore Durgha Sheriff","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Nagore_Durgha_Sheriff%2C_Georgetown-01.jpg/960px-Nagore_Durgha_Sheriff%2C_Georgetown-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of the Assumption (Penang)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.420762,"lo":100.337817,"d":852,"ds":"The Church of the Assumption is a Roman Catholic church within George Town in the Malaysian state of Penang. Established following the founding of George Town in 1786, it is the third oldest Catholic church in Malaysia. The church was also the seat of the bishop of Penang from 1955 to 2003.","wpu":"Church of the Assumption (Penang)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Church_of_the_Assumption%2C_Penang_%28I%29.jpg/960px-Church_of_the_Assumption%2C_Penang_%28I%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kapitan Keling Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.416667,"lo":100.337389,"d":855,"ds":"The Kapitan Keling Mosque (Malay: Masjid Kapitan Keling, Tamil: காப்பித்தான் கெலிங மசூதி, romanized: Kāppittāṉ Keliṅa Macūti) is a mosque built in the 19th century by Indian Muslim traders in George Town, Penang, Malaysia. It is situated on the corner of Buckingham Street (Lebuh Buckingham) and Pitt Street (Jalan Masjid Kapitan Keling).","wpu":"Kapitan Keling Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Kapitan_Keling_Mosque.jpg/960px-Kapitan_Keling_Mosque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sun Yat-sen Museum Penang","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.415761,"lo":100.336242,"d":1000,"ds":"The Sun Yat-sen Museum Penang (Malay: Muzium Sun Yat-sen Pulau Pinang; Chinese: 孫中山槟城基地博物館) formerly called the Sun Yat-sen Penang Base, is a museum in George Town, Penang, Malaysia. The museum is dedicated to Sun Yat-sen, a Chinese nationalist who established the Republic of China after his efforts in the Xinhai Revolution.","wpu":"Sun Yat-sen Museum Penang","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Sun_Yat-sen_Museum_Penang_%28I%29.jpg/960px-Sun_Yat-sen_Museum_Penang_%28I%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Eng Chuan Tong Tan Kongsi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.413184,"lo":100.336338,"d":1098,"ds":"Eng Chuan Tong Tan Kongsi (Chinese: 穎川堂陳公司; Pe̍h-ōe-jī: Éng-chhoan-tông Tân-kong-si) is a Hokkien clan house at Beach Street in George Town, Penang, Malaysia. It was founded in the early 19th century by a Tan family from the Fujian province of Zhangzhou in China.","wpu":"Eng Chuan Tong Tan Kongsi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Eng_Chuan_Tong_Tan_Kongsi.jpg/960px-Eng_Chuan_Tong_Tan_Kongsi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"MWE Plaza","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.4221,"lo":100.3353,"d":1166,"ds":"MWE Plaza is an office building within George Town in the Malaysian state of Penang. Located at the junction of Farquhar and Leith streets within the city's Central Business District (CBD), the 25-storey building was opened in 1996. It is owned by MWE Holdings, which was founded by Low Hock Peng, the father of international fugitive Jho Low.","wpu":"MWE Plaza","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Plaza_MWE_in_George_Town%2C_Penang.jpg/960px-Plaza_MWE_in_George_Town%2C_Penang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Protestant Cemetery, George Town","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.42255,"lo":100.333231,"d":1397,"ds":"The Old Protestant Cemetery (Malay: Tanah Perkuburan Protestan Lama), also known as Northam Road Cemetery, is a disused Protestant cemetery in George Town, Penang, Malaysia. After more than a century of neglect, it is now listed as a Class 1 Heritage Site and is maintained by the Penang Heritage Trust.","wpu":"Old Protestant Cemetery, George Town","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Francis-light-grave.jpg/960px-Francis-light-grave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sia Boey Urban Archaeological Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.4124,"lo":100.33352,"d":1415,"ds":"The Sia Boey Urban Archaeological Park, also colloquially known as the Sia Boey, is an urban archaeology park within the city of George Town in the Malaysian state of Penang. Located at Prangin Road, it is touted as the first Urban Archaeological Park in the country.","wpu":"Sia Boey Urban Archaeological Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Sia_Boey%2C_George_Town%2C_Penang.jpg/960px-Sia_Boey%2C_George_Town%2C_Penang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sri Kunj Bihari Temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.41932,"lo":100.33192,"d":1455,"ds":"Sri Kunj Bihari Temple is a Hindu temple within George Town in the Malaysian state of Penang. Established in 1835, it is the oldest Vaishnava temple in the state. It is dedicated to Krishna, who is worshiped in the form of Kunj Bihari with his chief consort Radha at the temple's central sanctum.","wpu":"Sri Kunj Bihari Temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Sri_Kunj_Bihari_Temple%2C_George_Town%2C_Penang%2C_Malaysia.jpg/960px-Sri_Kunj_Bihari_Temple%2C_George_Town%2C_Penang%2C_Malaysia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Penang House of Music","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":5.414,"lo":100.331,"d":1612,"ds":"The Penang House of Music (PHoM) is an interactive music gallery in George Town, Malaysia. It consists of a gallery, a performing/exhibition space, and a resource centre that documents Malaysia's musical heritage with a focus on Penang's music and musicians. It is located on the 4th floor of the ICT Digital Mall, Penang and covers 650 square metres.","wpu":"Penang House of Music","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Phom_logo_3_colours.jpg/960px-Phom_logo_3_colours.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Penang is a Malaysian state located on the northwest coast of Peninsular Malaysia. It is divided into two parts by the Strait of Malacca: Penang Island to the west, where the capital city George Town is located, and Seberang Perai on the mainland Malay Peninsula to the east. These two halves are physically connected by the Penang Bridge and the Second Penang Bridge.","wpu":"Penang","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Flag_of_Penang_%28Malaysia%29.svg/960px-Flag_of_Penang_%28Malaysia%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}